\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Seven minutes after Candid Criminal disappeared into blackout and accusations, Gerry Hardman left the developing Elvi concert with what remained of Clown Squad.\
Nobody paid much attention. The carpark was acquiring amplifiers by the minute and the first musicians were arguing over which one of them had been booked to headline a concert nobody had booked. Elvi wandered between vehicles carrying guitars, cables, eskies and sufficient quantities of alcohol to make any purposeful movement look accidental.\
Gerry and his people moved south through the compound in ones and twos, shedding the audience rather than appearing to leave it. Their wigs and capes were still useful; nobody questioned an Elvis who appeared to be searching for somewhere else to be.\
The townhouses thinned against the bush.\
Gerry checked the time.\
TIGER was where it was supposed to be.\
He saw nothing at first, which improved his opinion of them. Then a patch of scrub acquired an operative, followed by another, and Gerry recognised enough of the remaining team to stop looking for threats and start looking at what they had carried up the mountain.\
They looked appalling.\
Nine and a half hours through the valley followed by four hours lying low had removed whatever glamour PAPER recruitment material might once have attached to clandestine field work. Clothing was dark with sweat and dirt, exposed skin scratched, boots stained with mud, faces carrying the fixed expression of people who had discovered that an automatic rifle became substantially heavier every hour it was not fired.\
Their equipment looked better than they did.\
One of TIGER opened a pack.\
Gerry looked down at the rifles.\
\'93Tax me.\'94\
The operative began handing them out.\
Clown Squad ceased being funny remarkably quickly.\
Bouffant wigs remained. So did the jumpsuits, capes, rhinestones and several heroic pairs of sunglasses, because there was nowhere useful to put them. Beneath and among all that Elvis, magazines disappeared into belts and clothing, ammunition was divided, and rifles found the people for whom TIGER had carried the second half of its load across the Jamison Valley.\
Gerry checked one of the weapons and looked over the remnants of the two teams. TIGER had got the guns in from underneath. CLOWN had got the people in through the gate. Neither route alone would have achieved much.\
Together they had produced an armed PAPER unit inside Sublime Point without asking the UDF, the Shaktis or anybody else for permission.\
From the carpark came the thump of a bass amplifier being tested well beyond its manufacturer\'92s recommendations.\
Gerry listened for a moment.\
\'93Excellent,\'94 he said. \'93Let\'92s try not to ruin the concert.\'94\
